6001: \begin{proposition}[Formal-source gate instantiation]
6002: \label{prop:formal-source-gate-instantiation}
6003: For the current presentation package, the Lean-facing assertions in rows
6004: \path{pc3} and \path{pc4} of Table~\ref{tab:primary-claim-inventory} are accepted
6005: only as bounded contextual interfaces, not as accepted formal-source claims.  The
6006: accepted certificate surface is the traceability row $a_1$, the fake-extension
6007: boundary row $a_5$, Table~\ref{tab:source-interface-record},
6008: \path{source_interface_record.json},
6009: \path{review_bundle/primary_claim_inventory.json}, and
6010: \path{review_bundle/certificate_schema.json}.  These records certify that the
6011: short note may mention the mathlib-free design, marker-audit, and axiom-purity
6012: audit vocabulary only as source-interface context and only with the evidence
6013: boundary stated in rows \path{pc3} and \path{pc4}.  They do not certify a fresh
6014: Lean build, a current full-source marker audit, an absence-of-axioms theorem, or
6015: an accepted axiom-purity pass for the whole newmath tree.  Conversely, a
6016: Lean-facing sentence in this package can become a current accepted formal-source
6017: claim exactly by adding an accepted formal-source certificate row with build-gate
6018: and axiom-purity-gate pass records; if the sentence asserts freshness or currency
6019: of an execution, the row must also include the command, source commit or local
6020: state, environment, exit code, and log path required for command-run evidence.
6021: With the present records, every stronger reading of rows \path{pc3} and
6022: \path{pc4} has a minimal bad subrecord of missing formal-source gates or missing
6023: command-run fields.
6024: \end{proposition}
6025: 
6026: \begin{proof}
6027: By the dispatch rule in the operational interface, an accepted formal-source
6028: claim would have claim kind \emph{formal-source} and therefore would require
6029: pass records for both the build gate and the axiom-purity gate.  The current
6030: acceptance ledger has exactly the six rows $a_1,\ldots,a_6$ displayed in
6031: Table~\ref{tab:current-acceptance-ledger}; none has claim kind
6032: \emph{formal-source}.  The machine-readable schema mirrors this fact: its
6033: \path{claim_kind_domain} includes \path{formal-source}, and its
6034: \path{gate_dispatch.formal-source} field names \path{build} and
6035: \path{axiom-purity}, but the current pass-record ledger uses $a_1$ for
6036: traceability, $a_5$ for rejected stronger discovery/theorem-content claims, and
6037: contains no accepted formal-source row with build and axiom-purity pass records.
6038: 
6039: It follows that the only positive certificate available for rows \path{pc3} and
6040: \path{pc4} is contextual.  Row $a_1$ supplies inventory and marker-existence
6041: records for the pinned source-interface snapshot, so the paper may say which
6042: Lean-related source-interface vocabulary is being imported.  Row $a_5$ supplies
6043: the boundary that file churn, compilation, or a fake extension is not theorem
6044: growth or discovery, so the paper may explain why formal-looking material is not
6045: promoted without its gate records.  The certificate schema and primary-claim
6046: inventory make the boundary checkable by naming the absent stronger level: a
6047: formal-source acceptance would need build and axiom-purity pass records, and a
6048: command-run version would additionally need command, source commit, environment,
6049: exit code, and log path.  Since those fields are absent from the current package,
6050: Proposition~\ref{prop:finite-certificate-schema-verifier} rejects the stronger
6051: reading by a finite missing-gate or evidence-level subrecord.  The proposition
6052: therefore certifies exactly the bounded contextual Lean interface and no fresh
6053: Lean build or full-source axiom audit.
6054: 
6055: For the converse boundary, suppose a revised package did add a formal-source
6056: certificate row for one of these sentences.  By the deterministic dispatch rule,
6057: the necessary gate set for the formal-source component is precisely
6058: \(\{\texttt{build},\texttt{axiom-purity}\}\), so pass records for those two gates are
6059: sufficient for the finite schema verifier as far as the formal-source component is
6060: concerned, provided the other coordinates of the record meet the same evidence,
6061: promotion, discovery, and boundary checks as any accepted claim.  If the revised
6062: sentence also says that the build or audit was freshly executed for the current
6063: package, Definition~\ref{def:operational-acceptance-interface} adds exactly the
6064: five command-run fields.  Therefore the present package is not merely silent
6065: about currency: it is a complete negative instantiation of the formal-source
6066: gate, and currency can be claimed only by supplying the missing finite rows.\end{proof}
6067: 
6068: \begin{theorem}[Formal-source promotion obstruction]
6069: \label{thm:formal-source-promotion-obstruction}
6070: In the current presentation package, no manuscript sentence about the Lean/BEDC
6071: source interface can be promoted from bounded contextual interface evidence to an
6072: accepted formal-source claim unless the certificate is enlarged by an accepted
6073: formal-source row containing build-gate and axiom-purity-gate pass records.  If a
6074: fresh execution reading is also asserted, the certificate must additionally
6075: contain the command, source commit or final package digest manifest, environment, exit code, and log
6076: path required for command-run evidence.  With the present six-row package ledger,
6077: every such promotion attempt has a finite bad subrecord and is rejected by the
6078: schema verifier.
6079: \end{theorem}
6080: 
6081: \begin{proof}
6082: The dispatch table sends a formal-source claim to the two gates named in
6083: Proposition~\ref{prop:formal-source-gate-instantiation}: build and
6084: axiom-purity.  Therefore any accepted formal-source version of rows \path{pc3}
6085: or \path{pc4} would need a certificate whose kind set contains
6086: \emph{formal-source} and whose pass-record field $R$ contains one pass record for
6087: each of these two gates.  By
6088: Theorem~\ref{thm:current-package-certificate-exactness}, the current package has
6089: exactly the accepted certificates $a_1,\ldots,a_6$.  None has claim kind
6090: \emph{formal-source}; none contains build-gate and axiom-purity-gate pass records;
6091: and the only positive Lean-facing surface is the bounded traceability and
6092: source-interface context identified in
6093: Proposition~\ref{prop:formal-source-gate-instantiation}.
6094: 
6095: If the promoted reading also asserts a fresh Lean build, current full-source
6096: marker audit, full-source axiom audit, or command-run theorem-source check, then
6097: Definition~\ref{def:operational-acceptance-interface} requires the command-run
6098: tuple: command, source commit or final package digest manifest, environment, exit code, and log
6099: path.  The boundary fields in Tables~\ref{tab:current-certificate-ledger}
6100: and~\ref{tab:command-boundary-inventory} state that no such command-run tuple is
6101: present for the Lean/BEDC source theorem semantics.  Thus a formal-source
6102: promotion fails check~(iii) of the finite verifier by missing required gate pass
6103: records; a fresh-execution promotion fails check~(vi) by unsupported command-run
6104: escalation.  In either case Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-certificate-obstruction-skeleton}
6105: extracts the failed fields as a finite bad subrecord.  Hence the current package
6106: keeps the Lean/BEDC material at the declared bounded contextual level unless new
6107: formal-source and command-run evidence rows are actually added.\end{proof}
6108: 
6109: \begin{theorem}[Evidence-level overclaim obstruction]
6110: \label{thm:evidence-level-overclaim-obstruction}
6111: Fix the certificate schema, dispatch function, and evidence-level vocabulary of
6112: this paper.  Let $c$ be a load-bearing claim whose accepted certificate has
6113: level path-verified and whose boundary field $B$ records that no command-run
6114: assertion is made.  Then every stronger reading of $c$ that asserts any of the
6115: following additional conclusions is rejected by the finite schema verifier:
6116: \begin{enumerate}
6117: \item external gate soundness for an arbitrary implementation of the workflow;
6118: \item Lean correctness, full-source rebuild success, or axiom-purity beyond the
6119: recorded formal-source gate fields;
6120: \item end-to-end external semantic validity of artifact data beyond the finite
6121: manifest and evaluator surfaces named in $E$ and $R$;
6122: \item a fresh daemon run, full-source rerun, Rule110 or GroundCompiler rerun, or
6123: journal or workshop acceptance; or
6124: \item bundle-backed reviewability when neither \path{review_bundle/} nor an
6125: archival equivalent carrying the same finite records is in the submitted
6126: artifact boundary.
6127: \end{enumerate}
6128: More precisely, such a stronger reading has an inclusion-minimal bad subrecord of
6129: one of the five types in
6130: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-certificate-obstruction-skeleton}.  Hence the accepted
6131: claim is rigid at its declared evidence level: path-verified certificate records
6132: cannot be promoted by prose into command-run evidence, semantic validation,
6133: formal-source correctness, or external acceptance.
6134: \end{theorem}
6135: 
6136: \begin{proof}
6137: Let $c^+$ be one of the stronger readings.  It has the same displayed manuscript
6138: sentence as $c$ but a larger claim-kind set, a stronger evidence level, or a
6139: larger submitted-artifact boundary requirement.  The verifier does not inspect
6140: rhetorical emphasis; by Proposition~\ref{prop:finite-certificate-schema-verifier}
6141: it inspects only the finite tuple
6142: $\mathcal C=(K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B)$ and the finite dispatch table.
6143: 
6144: If $c^+$ asserts external gate soundness for an arbitrary implementation, then
6145: it is not merely the finite certificate-construction claim accepted by
6146: the finite-record interface theorem, Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface}.  It needs pass records showing
6147: that the relevant implementation ran every gate in
6148: $\bigcup_{k\in K}\Gamma(k)$.  Those records are not supplied by the
6149: path-verified certificate for $c$, so the minimal bad subrecord is a missing
6150: pass-record obstruction of type~2.  If $c^+$ asserts Lean correctness,
6151: full-source rebuild success, or axiom-purity, then it is a formal-source or
6152: command-run claim.  Proposition~\ref{prop:formal-source-gate-instantiation} and
6153: Theorem~\ref{thm:formal-source-promotion-obstruction} identify the required
6154: build, axiom-purity, and command-run fields; when they are absent, the minimal
6155: bad subrecord is type~2 for missing formal-source gates or type~5 for command-run
6156: escalation.
6157: 
6158: If $c^+$ asserts end-to-end semantic validity of artifacts beyond the named
6159: manifest and evaluator surfaces, then it has an artifact component not covered
6160: by the finite status and limitation-ledger records in $R$.  The current
6161: acceptance and certificate ledgers display only the bounded artifact row $a_6$:
6162: its evidence surface is the Rule110 status file, limitation ledgers,
6163: paper-data reference, and review-bundle manifest, and its boundary field says
6164: that no fresh artifact rerun is asserted.  A stronger artifact claim therefore
6165: lacks an assigned artifact pass record, giving type~2,
6166: or asserts a fresh evaluator run without command-run fields, giving type~5.  The
6167: same argument covers a fresh daemon run, full-source rerun, and Rule110 or
6168: GroundCompiler rerun: each is a command-run or artifact-strength escalation and
6169: is rejected by the missing gate or command-run boundary fields.
6170: 
6171: If $c^+$ asserts journal or workshop acceptance, then its acceptance-entry field
6172: would have to contain an editorial or venue acceptance record.  The operational
6173: notion of acceptance entry in this paper is internal and submission-local; the
6174: current ledger contains no external editorial acceptance row.  Thus the stronger
6175: reading either lacks the required route/submission pass record, giving type~2,
6176: or lacks the relevant external evidence surface, giving type~1.  Finally, if a
6177: bundle-backed claim is made while no review bundle or archival equivalent lies
6178: inside the submitted artifact boundary, the evidence item named by the
6179: certificate is absent from $E$ at the declared level, so the minimal bad
6180: subrecord is type~1.  These cases exhaust the displayed stronger readings.
6181: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-certificate-obstruction-skeleton} then gives the stated
6182: classification and proves rigidity at the declared evidence level.\end{proof}
6183: 
6184: \begin{theorem}[Finite-witness manifest-bounded record interface]
6185: \label{thm:finite-witness-manifest-record-interface}
6186: This theorem is a manifest-bounded finite-record interface instance of
6187: Theorem~\ref{thm:evidence-level-overclaim-obstruction}: it records only the
6188: bounded manifest/evaluator coordinates that may be checked, and it rejects
6189: stronger artifact readings unless the package supplies the additional finite
6190: records required for them, such as semantic-validation pass records or fresh
6191: rerun logs.  The interface domain is the finite set
6192: \[
6193: \mathcal M_{a_6}=\{\text{manifest entry},\text{local paths},
6194: \text{reviewable snapshots},\text{boundary field},
6195: \text{status pass row},\text{limitation-ledger pass row}\}.
6196: \]
6197: No Rule110 or GroundCompiler property outside $\mathcal M_{a_6}$ is asserted or certified; semantic validation outside this finite set requires a new artifact or evaluator-command certificate.
6198: 
6199: For the current presentation package, assuming the named review-bundle records
6200: are supplied as supplemental material or by an accepted archive-equivalent
6201: surface, the finite-witness assertions in rows
6202: \path{pc5}, \path{pc6}, and \path{pc11} of
6203: Table~\ref{tab:primary-claim-inventory} have the following bounded interface.
6204: The assertions printed in this source are manuscript-source assertions about the
6205: allowed finite-record reading; the path checks against the status files,
6206: limitation ledgers, and machine-readable manifest are supplemental-record
6207: assertions conditional on the named \path{review_bundle/} records being submitted
6208: and matching those paths.
6209: Row \path{pc5} is contextual: it states the evidence discipline that finite
6210: witnesses are bound by manifest rows, evaluator outcomes, and substrate
6211: contracts rather than by Lean alone.  Rows \path{pc6} and \path{pc11} are
6212: accepted only as the bounded artifact lesson $a_6$.  Their named manifest and
6213: evaluator surfaces are \path{manifest:case_evidence[3]}, its
6214: \path{local_paths}, \path{reviewable_snapshots}, and \path{boundary} fields,
6215: the \path{status} and \path{limitation-ledger} pass records for $a_6$ in
6216: \path{review_bundle/current_package_pass_records.json},
6217: \path{review_bundle/case_snapshots/rule110_STATUS.md},
6218: \path{review_bundle/case_snapshots/rule110_collision_audit_findings.md},
6219: \path{review_bundle/case_snapshots/rule110_exhaustiveness_audit_findings.md},
6220: and \path{review_bundle/case_snapshots/rule110_papers_research_report.md}.
6221: Thus the Rule110/GroundCompiler statement is a finite manifest-bounded artifact
6222: status and limitation-ledger claim, not a claim of Rule110 universality, a fresh
6223: evaluator run, a dynamic behavior theorem, or an unbounded artifact validation.
6224: Its classification is path-complete only for the named finite records in
6225: $\mathcal M_{a_6}$.  Moreover this bounded interface is invariant under
6226: manifest-record equivalence: any two review states
6227: that agree on the named manifest entry, reviewable snapshots, pass-record rows,
6228: and boundary field receive the same publication certificate from this package.
6229: Therefore no additional Rule110/GroundCompiler artifact property can be derived
6230: from the current certificate unless it is already expressible as one of those
6231: finite manifest-bounded fields or a new artifact/command-run certificate is
6232: added.  In particular, any stronger Rule110 or GroundCompiler reading asserting a
6233: fresh evaluator outcome, semantic validation, dynamic behavior, collision-audit
6234: rerun, exhaustiveness rerun, or universality-level artifact fact requires fresh
6235: evaluator logs with command, source state, environment, exit code, and log path;
6236: manifest rows and historical snapshots alone do not supply such a claim.  The
6237: theorem is exactly a finite-record boundary theorem for the artifact
6238: row $a_6$ in the finite-record interface theorem, Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface}: it preserves the
6239: bounded artifact-limitation lesson and classifies all readings requiring further
6240: artifact evidence as outside the current finite certificate.
6241: \end{theorem}
6242: 
6243: \begin{proof}
6244: The finite-witness vocabulary in \path{pc5} is not itself an artifact pass: it
6245: describes which objects would bind a finite substrate claim.  Under the schema
6246: verifier, such a statement becomes load-bearing only when a finite artifact
6247: certificate supplies the assigned artifact gates.  The dispatch function sends
6248: artifact claims to the status and limitation-ledger gates.  In the current
6249: ledger, the only artifact entry is $a_6$, and
6250: Table~\ref{tab:current-certificate-ledger} gives its evidence surface: the Rule110
6251: status file, collision and exhaustiveness ledgers, paper-data reference, and the
6252: review-bundle manifest.  The same row records the boundary excluding a fresh
6253: \path{make test-collision-audit} or dynamic artifact rerun.  Therefore the only
6254: coordinates available to the artifact certificate are precisely the finite
6255: coordinates collected in $\mathcal M_{a_6}$.
6256: 
6257: The review bundle supplies a finite external-review surface for precisely that
6258: artifact row.  Proposition~\ref{prop:review-bundle-case-snapshot-coverage}
6259: identifies the Rule110 case manifest entry with four reviewable snapshots: the
6260: status file, the collision-audit findings, the exhaustiveness-audit findings,
6261: and the papers research report.  The current pass-record ledger names the two
6262: gate records used by $a_6$: a \path{status} pass record whose input surface is
6263: the Rule110 status file and paper-data reference, and a
6264: \path{limitation-ledger} pass record whose input surface is the collision and
6265: exhaustiveness audit ledgers.  These are the manifest and evaluator-facing fields
6266: available to the current package, and they are exactly the concrete
6267: representatives of $\mathcal M_{a_6}$.  They are finite strings and bundled
6268: snapshots, so Proposition~\ref{prop:finite-certificate-schema-verifier} can check
6269: them without interpreting generated prose, rerunning an evaluator, or assigning
6270: semantics to Rule110 or GroundCompiler beyond the recorded manifest/evaluator
6271: boundary.
6272: 
6273: No other artifact entry is present.  Therefore any stronger reading of
6274: \path{pc6} or \path{pc11}---for example, that the Rule110 artifact suite was
6275: freshly rerun, that GroundCompiler is semantically validated beyond the manifest
6276: family, or that Lean supplies an unbounded semantic universe for the artifact
6277: boundary---would require a further artifact certificate or command-run fields.
6278: Those fields are absent, and the schema verifier rejects the stronger reading by
6279: a finite missing-record obstruction.  The accepted finite-witness conclusion is
6280: exactly the bounded $a_6$ artifact lesson.
6281: 
6282: It remains to prove the manifest-record invariance assertion.  Let $W$ and $W'$
6283: be two possible review states, for example two submitted bundles or two local
6284: working trees, that agree on the finite coordinates named in the statement: the
6285: manifest entry, the four reviewable snapshots, the two pass-record rows for
6286: $a_6$, and the boundary excluding fresh reruns.  The certificate constructor and
6287: the schema verifier never query any other coordinate of a review state;
6288: equivalently, these are exactly the artifact coordinates of the finite set $F$
6289: from
6290: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary}.  They therefore build the same tuple
6291: \((K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B)\) for $W$ and for $W'$, and
6292: Proposition~\ref{prop:finite-certificate-schema-verifier} returns the same
6293: accepted bounded-artifact decision in both cases.  A dynamic semantic assertion
6294: that could hold in $W$ and fail in $W'$ is not a function of this finite tuple.
6295: It cannot be a consequence of the current publication certificate.  To make such
6296: an assertion load-bearing, the package would have to add a new finite coordinate:
6297: an artifact pass record for the asserted semantic property, or a command-run tuple
6298: recording the relevant evaluator execution.  This proves manifest-record
6299: invariance for the finite-witness manifest interface and shows that the theorem is
6300: only a finite-record boundary result for row $a_6$ and the coordinate set
6301: $\mathcal M_{a_6}$, not an artifact-validation
6302: theorem.\end{proof}
6303: 
6304: \begin{proposition}[Artifact-validation gate is the unique semantic upgrade]
6305: \label{prop:artifact-validation-gate-unique-semantic-upgrade}
6306: For a finite-witness artifact claim certified through the manifest-bounded
6307: coordinate set $\mathcal M_{a_6}$ of
6308: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-witness-manifest-record-interface}, any strengthening to
6309: dynamic artifact semantics, unbounded evaluator correctness, or semantic
6310: Rule110/GroundCompiler validity factors through a separate artifact-validation
6311: gate.  Such a gate must add, at minimum, the evaluator contract, the artifact
6312: bytes or manifest row being validated, the semantic predicate checked, and either
6313: an artifact pass record or a command-run tuple with source state, environment,
6314: command, exit code, and log path.  Without those added coordinates, the
6315: manifest/evaluator-record certificate is maximal among finite-record readings and
6316: the semantic strengthening is rejected as a finite bad subrecord.
6317: \end{proposition}
6318: 
6319: \begin{proof}
6320: The proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-witness-manifest-record-interface} shows that
6321: the certificate constructor for row $a_6$ reads only the manifest entry, the four
6322: reviewable snapshots, the two pass-record rows, and the boundary excluding fresh
6323: reruns.  It never queries a live evaluator, an unbounded artifact semantics, or a
6324: semantic correctness proof.  Hence two review states that agree on
6325: $\mathcal M_{a_6}$ but differ in evaluator behavior or semantic validity produce
6326: the same finite certificate.  By
6327: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary}, no semantic assertion
6328: that can distinguish those two states is determined by the current certificate.
6329: 
6330: The displayed artifact-validation coordinates are exactly the finite data needed
6331: to make the semantic assertion a record claim: they identify the evaluator
6332: contract, the artifact input, the predicate being checked, and the pass or
6333: command-result evidence for that check.  If any coordinate is missing,
6334: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-certificate-obstruction-skeleton} returns it as the bad
6335: subrecord.  If all are present with the assigned pass records, the strengthening
6336: is an accepted finite extension by
6337: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-extension-budget-normal-form}.  Thus the only route from
6338: the present finite-witness certificate to dynamic artifact semantics is a
6339: separate logged artifact-validation gate, and the existing manifest certificate
6340: is maximal until that gate is supplied.
6341: \end{proof}
6342: 
6343: \begin{corollary}[Artifact semantic-validity boundary for finite witnesses]
6344: \label{cor:artifact-semantic-validity-boundary}
6345: Let $c$ be a finite-witness artifact claim in the current package whose accepted
6346: certificate uses the artifact row $a_6$, and let
6347: $\mathcal M_{a_6}$ be the manifest-bounded coordinate set of
6348: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-witness-manifest-record-interface}.  If a proposed
6349: stronger reading $c^{\mathrm{sem}}$ asserts semantic validity of the unbounded
6350: Rule110/GroundCompiler artifact, semantic correctness of a dynamic evaluator, or
6351: artifact validity beyond the exact evaluator contract recorded in
6352: $\mathcal M_{a_6}$, then $c^{\mathrm{sem}}$ is not certified by the present
6353: manuscript package.  It becomes a certifiable reading only after the package adds
6354: one of the following finite coordinates:
6355: \begin{enumerate}
6356: \item an artifact pass record whose input surface is the exact evaluator
6357: contract and whose boundary field states the semantic property being checked; or
6358: \item a command-run tuple for the evaluator, including source state, environment,
6359: command, exit code, and log path, together with the manifest row tying that run to
6360: the submitted artifact.
6361: \end{enumerate}
6362: In either case the added coordinate must be an artifact-validation interface, not
6363: a reinterpretation of the existing manifest: it must state the evaluator contract,
6364: the artifact bytes or manifest row to which the contract is applied, the checked
6365: semantic predicate, and the pass record or command-result record that witnesses
6366: that exact validation.
6367: Consequently, the assertions in primary rows \path{pc5}, \path{pc6}, and
6368: \path{pc11} that finite substrates are bounded by manifest rows, evaluator
6369: outcomes, and substrate contracts are valid only as finite-record interface
6370: assertions.  For the Rule110/GroundCompiler case they do not assert unbounded
6371: semantic validity unless the exact evaluator contract and its assigned pass or
6372: command-run record are present on the submitted support surface.
6373: \end{corollary}
6374: 
6375: \begin{proof}
6376: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-witness-manifest-record-interface} identifies the complete
6377: finite coordinate set available to row $a_6$: the manifest entry, local paths,
6378: reviewable snapshots, boundary field, status pass row, and limitation-ledger pass
6379: row.  Its proof also shows manifest-record invariance: two review states agreeing
6380: on those coordinates produce the same artifact certificate even if they differ in
6381: unrecorded dynamic or semantic facts.  Therefore semantic validity of an
6382: unbounded Rule110/GroundCompiler artifact is not a function of the accepted tuple
6383: unless the semantic evaluator contract itself appears as one of the submitted
6384: finite coordinates.
6385: 
6386: Equivalently, compare the accepted artifact reading $c$ with the stronger reading
6387: $c^{\mathrm{sem}}$ in the preorder of
6388: Definition~\ref{def:stronger-reading-preorder}.  The stronger reading enlarges at
6389: least one of the typed fields: it adds an artifact-semantic evidence surface, a
6390: new pass record for the evaluator contract, or a command-run evidence level for a
6391: fresh evaluator execution.  By Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness}, an
6392: accepted stronger reading must satisfy the same finite schema after that
6393: enlargement.  If the package supplies neither an artifact pass record for the
6394: semantic evaluator contract nor a command-run tuple with source state,
6395: environment, command, exit code, and log path, then the enlarged certificate has
6396: a finite bad subrecord: either the required evaluator pass row is absent from
6397: $R$, or the command-run coordinate required at evidence level $\mathsf{command}$
6398: is absent from $B$.  Theorem~\ref{thm:mixed-claim-projection-obstruction} then
6399: prevents the status and limitation-ledger rows from certifying the omitted
6400: semantic component.
6401: 
6402: The extra sentence in the statement only unpacks what such an extension must
6403: contain.  Without an evaluator contract and an artifact-byte or manifest-row
6404: binding, the proposed semantic predicate is not assigned to a finite artifact;
6405: without a pass record or command-result record, it is not accepted at the declared
6406: evidence level.  Thus an artifact-semantics claim routes to a separate
6407: artifact-validation interface rather than to the historical manifest row.
6408: 
6409: For the current package, rows \path{pc5}, \path{pc6}, and \path{pc11} are tied to
6410: the bounded artifact lesson $a_6$ and to the finite surfaces listed in
6411: $\mathcal M_{a_6}$.  Those surfaces include manifest and evaluator-facing status
6412: or limitation records, but the command-boundary inventory expressly lacks a fresh
6413: Rule110/GroundCompiler semantic rerun or dynamic evaluator log.  Hence the only
6414: certified conclusion is the finite-record one stated in the corollary.  Any
6415: unbounded semantic-validity claim requires one of the additional finite
6416: coordinates enumerated above, so it lies outside the current certificate.\end{proof}
