6436: \begin{theorem}[Primary-to-support dependency completeness]
6437: \label{thm:primary-to-support-dependency-completeness}
6438: Assume that the primary presentation source \path{submission_abstract.tex}, the
6439: technical supplement \path{main.tex}, the primary-claim inventory
6440: \path{review_bundle/primary_claim_inventory.json}, and the support map in
6441: Table~\ref{tab:primary-claim-support-map} are included in the same finite support
6442: surface.  Then every load-bearing primary row \path{pc1},\ldots,\path{pc12}
6443: factors through one of the support classes displayed in
6444: Table~\ref{tab:primary-claim-support-map}.  More precisely, the support map is a
6445: finite surjection from the twelve primary rows onto the five displayed support
6446: classes, and each class is discharged only at the evidence level of the named
6447: support labels and files.  No primary row obtains an additional theorem, source,
6448: Lean, artifact-semantic, fresh-rerun, upload, archive, or venue-acceptance claim
6449: from the compact primary prose alone.
6450: \end{theorem}
6451: 
6452: \begin{proof}
6453: The domain of the map is the finite row set printed in
6454: \path{review_bundle/primary_claim_inventory.json} and summarized in
6455: Table~\ref{tab:primary-claim-inventory}: the rows are exactly
6456: \path{pc1},\ldots,\path{pc12}.  Table~\ref{tab:primary-claim-support-map}
6457: partitions that finite set into five nonempty blocks: rows \path{pc1},
6458: \path{pc8}, and \path{pc9}; rows \path{pc2} and \path{pc7}; rows
6459: \path{pc3} and \path{pc4}; rows \path{pc5}, \path{pc6}, and \path{pc11}; and
6460: rows \path{pc10} and \path{pc12}.  Hence it is a finite surjection onto the five
6461: support classes.
6462: 
6463: For the first block, the only support labels named by the map are the
6464: finite-record publication-safety and current-package exactness results,
6465: Theorems~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface} and
6466: \ref{thm:current-package-certificate-exactness}, together with their executable
6467: schema and acceptance-ledger records.  Those results are explicitly conditional
6468: on record-gate soundness and finite evidence surfaces, so they do not certify
6469: gate implementation soundness or semantic truth of every assertion.  For the
6470: second block, Lemma~\ref{lem:source-interface-extraction-correctness} and the
6471: source-interface propositions give only the bounded path, commit, line-range,
6472: label, snapshot, and evidence-level interface; by
6473: Theorem~\ref{thm:bounded-source-interface-maximality} this does not become a
6474: full-source semantic-completeness or fresh Lean check.  For the third block,
6475: Proposition~\ref{prop:formal-source-gate-instantiation} and
6476: Theorem~\ref{thm:formal-source-promotion-obstruction} classify Lean-facing rows
6477: as separate verification-axis records, not as proof-assistant acceptance or
6478: object-language closure claims.
6479: 
6480: For the fourth block, Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-witness-manifest-record-interface}
6481: and Corollary~\ref{cor:artifact-semantic-validity-boundary} restrict
6482: finite-witness and case-evidence rows to manifest, evaluator-contract,
6483: snapshot, limitation-ledger, and boundary coordinates.  They expressly require a
6484: separate artifact-validation pass or command-run tuple for unbounded artifact
6485: semantics or fresh evaluator execution.  For the fifth block,
6486: Proposition~\ref{prop:primary-supplement-interface-map},
6487: Theorem~\ref{thm:support-surface-normal-form}, and
6488: Corollary~\ref{cor:current-no-receipt-support-surface-non-escalation} classify
6489: package and route assertions as exact-inclusion or archive-equivalent support
6490: claims, conditional on the displayed finite records and not on an unrecorded
6491: upload, DOI, archive, or venue-acceptance event.
6492: 
6493: Thus each primary row is routed to a named support class whose theorem labels and
6494: files determine the exact evidence level.  If a reader attempts to strengthen any
6495: row beyond that class, the strengthened reading adds a coordinate outside the
6496: finite block assigned to the row.  The finite-coordinate determination theorem,
6497: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary}, and the bad-subrecord
6498: skeleton, Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-certificate-obstruction-skeleton}, then return
6499: the missing support coordinate as the obstruction.  Therefore the compact primary
6500: article is completely mapped to the supplement and review-bundle support surface